    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d27">

                <pb facs="0024" n="20" sameAs="frus:frus1938v04/pg20"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.515/1313: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The First Secretary of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Salisbury</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-05-27T16:00:00+08:00">May 27, 1938—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received May 28—6 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>330. Embassy’s 320, May 25, 5 p.m.<anchor n="29" xml:id="d27-f1"/> An undated
                    proclamation carried by today’s press over the signature of Wang Keh Min<anchor
                        n="30" xml:id="d27-f2"/> prohibits circulation, after June 10, of notes of
                    Banks of China and Communications except for North China issued [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">issues?</hi>] and of the following banks: Land Bank of
                    China, Commercial Guarantee Bank of Chihli, China Development Bank, National
                    Commercial Bank, Tah Chung Bank, Frontier Bank, Agricultural and Industrial Bank
                    of China, Central Bank of China, China and South Seas Bank, Commercial Bank of
                    China, Bank of Agriculture and Commerce, Ningpo Commercial and Savings Bank,
                    Farmers Bank of China.</p>
                <pb facs="0025" n="21" xml:id="pg21"/>
                <p>At end of proclamation there is admonition to holders of such notes to take steps
                    to change them from [<hi rendition="#italic">for?</hi>] Federal Reserve Bank
                    notes before expiration of the time limit “in order to avoid unexpected losses”.
                    It is stated also that such notes are not to be given or received after June 10
                    but that this does not apply to the receipt by the various banks of their own
                    notes.</p>
                <p>Sent to Ambassador [at] Hankow, Shanghai, by mail to Tokyo and Tientsin.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Salisbury</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
